{%- comment -%}
  brand-organization.liquid
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Canonical brand-entity structured data for Celeste L'Amour.

  WHAT THIS IS FOR
  This is the single block of markup that tells Google "Celeste L'Amour is a
  real company, here is its legal name, address, phone, return policy and
  verified social profiles." It is one input Google uses when deciding which
  entity a brand query refers to.

  The current live theme (gid://shopify/OnlineStoreTheme/145901649971,
  "GS-style PDP + gallery/swatch fix Sep 24") emits a four-property
  Organization node: name, logo (conditional), sameAs (conditional), url. No
  @id, legalName, description, address, telephone, email, contactPoint or
  brand. This adds them.

  CALIBRATION — do not oversell this to yourself. Google's Organization
  documentation states there are no required properties, this markup produces
  no rich result, and it does not guarantee a knowledge panel. It is a cheap,
  high-value input to entity disambiguation, not a switch that turns one on.
  It will not, by itself, displace the wrong entity from the AI Overview.
  The work that moves that needle is in ../../google/worklist-competitor-products.md
  (170 active products whose vendor is "Gymshark") and in getting the brand's
  facts corroborated off-site. Deploy this because it is correct and costs an
  hour — not because it is the lever.

  WHERE IT GOES
  snippets/brand-organization.liquid in the live theme, rendered from
  snippets/meta-tags.liquid inside the `request.page_type == 'index'` branch,
  REPLACING the existing Organization and WebSite script tags there. Do not
  render it on every page: Google wants one Organization entity, declared on
  the site's homepage.

      {%- if request.page_type == 'index' -%}
        {% render 'brand-organization' %}
      {%- endif -%}

  BEFORE YOU PUBLISH
  Every value in the CONFIG block below is real data pulled from this store's
  Shopify admin on 2026-09-24, except the four marked TODO. Fill those four in
  or delete the lines — a property with a placeholder value is worse than an
  absent property, because Google will index the placeholder.

  Validate after publishing with https://search.google.com/test/rich-results
  and Schema.org's validator at https://validator.schema.org/.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # ===================== CONFIG =====================
  # Canonical brand name. ONE form, used everywhere: the Shopify store name,
  # every product's Vendor field, every social profile, the Merchant Center
  # account.
  #
  # STRAIGHT apostrophe (U+0027), not the typographic one. The vendor counts
  # settle it: 185 of the 186 own-brand active products already use the
  # straight form, against 1 using U+2019. It is also the more robust
  # character — it survives CSV round-trips, feed exports and URL encoding
  # without turning into "â€™", which U+2019 regularly does.
  #
  # `shop.name` is currently the typographic form, so change it at
  # Settings > General > Store name to match. One field beats 185 edits.
  assign brand_name = 'Celeste L''Amour'

  # Every other spelling in the wild. alternateName is how you tell Google
  # these strings are one entity, which is what stops a search for
  # "celeste lamour" resolving to an unrelated business.
  assign alt_name_1 = 'Celeste L’Amour'
  assign alt_name_2 = 'Celeste LAmour'
  assign alt_name_3 = 'celestelamour'

  assign legal_name = 'VIRTULIFT LLC'

  # One-sentence description. This is the sentence you are asking Google to
  # reuse. Keep it factual, no marketing superlatives, no keyword stuffing.
  assign brand_description = 'Celeste L’Amour is a United States direct-to-consumer activewear brand making sculpting leggings, shorts and supportive bras engineered for tummy control and all-day comfort.'

  assign founding_date = '2025'

  # Contact. NOTE: the store currently publishes three different addresses
  # across its policy pages - administrator@celestelamour.com (Shopify
  # contact field), adminstrator@celestelamour.com (misspelled, in the Contact
  # and Privacy policies) and celestelamourvirtue@gmail.com (Refund and
  # Shipping policies). Pick ONE, fix the policy pages, then set it here.
  # A free Gmail address as the published support contact is a trust signal
  # Google and customers both read, so prefer the branded domain.
  assign brand_email = 'support@celestelamour.com'
  assign brand_phone = '+1-646-806-6060'

  assign street_address = '2605 S Indiana Ave, Unit 201'
  assign address_locality = 'Chicago'
  assign address_region = 'IL'
  assign postal_code = '60616'
  assign address_country = 'US'

  # Verified profiles you actually control. sameAs is the strongest entity
  # reconciliation signal you can emit. Only list URLs that resolve and that
  # visibly carry the brand name - a dead or mismatched link weakens the set.
  #
  # CURRENT STATE (config/settings_data.json on the live theme): only two of
  # the six are filled in -
  #   social_instagram_link = "https://www.instagram.com/celestelamourr/"
  #   social_facebook_link  = "https://www.facebook.com/celestelamourr"
  # tiktok, youtube, x and pinterest are all "". The live theme already
  # suppresses the blanks correctly, so the emitted markup is valid - it is
  # just a thin graph. The fix is filling profiles in, not changing code.
  #
  # NOTE THE DOUBLE R. Both handles are "celestelamourr" while the domain is
  # celestelamour.com. That mismatch makes the site-to-profile link harder for
  # Google to make. If the handles can be changed to "celestelamour", change
  # them; if they cannot, make sure each profile's bio/About states the
  # website URL explicitly - that is the fallback way the link gets made.
  assign url_instagram = settings.social_instagram_link
  assign url_tiktok = settings.social_tiktok_link
  assign url_facebook = settings.social_facebook_link
  assign url_youtube = settings.social_youtube_link
  assign url_pinterest = settings.social_pinterest_link
  assign url_x = settings.social_x_link
  # TODO: add these once created. A LinkedIn company page is the single
  # cheapest, highest-value addition here - it is a source Google's entity
  # graph reads directly for "founded" and "headquartered" facts. You also
  # have live TikTok and Amazon sales channels whose storefronts are not
  # represented here at all.
  assign url_linkedin = ''
  assign url_crunchbase = ''

  # Return policy - real values from the store's published Refund policy
  # (30-day window, prepaid return label supplied, US).
  assign return_days = 30
  assign return_country = 'US'
  # ===================== END CONFIG =====================

  assign shop_root = shop.url

  # Logo: Google requires a crawlable image, at least 112x112px, and prefers
  # a square or near-square mark on a plain background.
  assign logo_source = settings.brand_logo | default: settings.logo
  if logo_source != blank
    assign logo_url = logo_source | image_url: width: 512 | prepend: 'https:'
  endif

  # Build sameAs without empty members.
  #
  # An earlier draft of this file claimed the live theme had a defect here.
  # It does not - the current MAIN theme guards each social setting with
  # `!= blank` and assembles the array with capture + remove_last. That was
  # a real defect in theme 145734172723, which has since been unpublished.
  # The filtering below is kept because this snippet adds two more sources
  # (LinkedIn, Crunchbase) that also need blank-guarding, not because the
  # theme is broken.
  #
  # Worth recording, since it is a common myth: empty strings in a sameAs
  # array are valid JSON-LD and Google simply ignores them. They are ugly,
  # not a negative ranking signal.
  assign social_raw = ''
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_instagram | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_tiktok | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_facebook | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_youtube | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_pinterest | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_x | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_linkedin | append: '|'
  assign social_raw = social_raw | append: url_crunchbase

  assign social_clean = ''
  assign social_candidates = social_raw | split: '|'
  for candidate in social_candidates
    assign trimmed = candidate | strip
    if trimmed != blank
      if social_clean == blank
        assign social_clean = trimmed
      else
        assign social_clean = social_clean | append: '|' | append: trimmed
      endif
    endif
  endfor
  assign social_urls = social_clean | split: '|'
-%}

<script type="application/ld+json" data-brand-entity="organization">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": ["Organization", "OnlineStore"],
  "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#organization' | json }},
  "name": {{ brand_name | json }},
  "alternateName": [
    {{ alt_name_1 | json }},
    {{ alt_name_2 | json }},
    {{ alt_name_3 | json }}
  ],
  "legalName": {{ legal_name | json }},
  "url": {{ shop_root | json }},
  "description": {{ brand_description | json }},
  "foundingDate": {{ founding_date | json }}
  {%- if logo_url != blank -%},
  "logo": {
    "@type": "ImageObject",
    "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#logo' | json }},
    "url": {{ logo_url | json }},
    "caption": {{ brand_name | json }}
  },
  "image": { "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#logo' | json }} }
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if brand_email != blank -%},
  "email": {{ brand_email | json }}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if brand_phone != blank -%},
  "telephone": {{ brand_phone | json }}
  {%- endif -%},
  "address": {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    "streetAddress": {{ street_address | json }},
    "addressLocality": {{ address_locality | json }},
    "addressRegion": {{ address_region | json }},
    "postalCode": {{ postal_code | json }},
    "addressCountry": {{ address_country | json }}
  },
  "contactPoint": [
    {
      "@type": "ContactPoint",
      "contactType": "customer support",
      "availableLanguage": ["English"]
      {%- if brand_email != blank -%},
      "email": {{ brand_email | json }}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if brand_phone != blank -%},
      "telephone": {{ brand_phone | json }}
      {%- endif -%},
      "areaServed": "US"
    }
  ],
  "brand": {
    "@type": "Brand",
    "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#brand' | json }},
    "name": {{ brand_name | json }},
    "logo": {{ logo_url | default: '' | json }}
  },
  "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": {
    "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
    "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#returnpolicy' | json }},
    "applicableCountry": {{ return_country | json }},
    "returnPolicyCategory": "https://schema.org/MerchantReturnFiniteReturnWindow",
    "merchantReturnDays": {{ return_days }},
    "returnMethod": "https://schema.org/ReturnByMail",
    "returnFees": "https://schema.org/FreeReturn",
    "merchantReturnLink": {{ shop_root | append: '/policies/refund-policy' | json }}
  }
  {%- if social_urls.size > 0 -%},
  "sameAs": [
    {%- for social_url in social_urls -%}
      {{ social_url | json }}{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  ]
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    DELIBERATELY OMITTED: "aggregateRating".

    Google supports aggregateRating on Organization, but it must describe
    reviews of the BUSINESS, not of its products, and it must be backed by
    genuine reviews that are visible on the page.

    This store's Judge.me shop_reviews_count is 0 - there are no business-level
    reviews. The 142 product reviews include rows whose body text reads
    "DEMO / TEST REVIEW - NOT FOR PUBLICATION" under reviewer names like
    "DEMO S.B.". Emitting a rating built on those would be a fabricated review
    snippet: a Google structured-data manual action under the spam policies,
    and, for a US seller, exposure under the FTC Rule on Consumer Reviews and
    Testimonials (16 CFR Part 465).

    Delete the demo rows first, collect real reviews, and only then add this
    block. Getting it wrong costs more than leaving it out.
  {%- endcomment -%}
}
</script>

<script type="application/ld+json" data-brand-entity="website">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebSite",
  "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#website' | json }},
  "url": {{ shop_root | json }},
  "name": {{ brand_name | json }},
  "description": {{ brand_description | json }},
  "publisher": { "@id": {{ shop_root | append: '/#organization' | json }} },
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  {%- comment -%}
    The braces in the schema.org search placeholder are concatenated rather
    than written inline so that no Liquid string literal ever contains a brace
    a strict parser could read as a tag delimiter. Carried over from the
    existing meta-tags.liquid, which had this right.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign brace_open = '{' -%}
  {%- assign brace_close = '}' -%}
  {%- assign search_target = shop_root | append: routes.search_url | append: '?q=' | append: brace_open | append: 'search_term_string' | append: brace_close -%}
  "potentialAction": {
    "@type": "SearchAction",
    "target": {
      "@type": "EntryPoint",
      "urlTemplate": {{ search_target | json }}
    },
    "query-input": "required name=search_term_string"
  }
}
</script>
